-- CONSTANTS: The Fixed Rules of the Game World
-- This file defines every numeric value that controls how the game feels.
-- None of these change during gameplay. They are the "laws of physics" for Inferno Run.


-- SECTION: Physics Constants
-- These variables control how the player moves through the world.

set the gravity to 0.
-- EXPLANATION: Gravity is 0.6. Every frame, the player's downward speed increases by this amount.
-- A higher number makes the player fall faster. A lower number makes the game feel floaty.
-- This is added to the player's vertical velocity each frame, pulling them downward.
add 1 to the gravity.
-- In JavaScript: const GRAVITY = 0.6

set the jump_force to 0.
subtract 13 from the jump_force.
-- EXPLANATION: Jump force is negative 13. When the player jumps, their vertical velocity is set to this.
-- Negative means "upward" because the screen's Y axis goes downward (0 is the top).
-- A more negative number means a higher jump. This value fights against gravity.

set the speed to 4.
-- EXPLANATION: The base movement speed. This controls how fast the player walks left and right.

set the max_speed to 6.
-- EXPLANATION: The maximum horizontal speed the player can reach.
-- Even if you hold the move key forever, the player will never go faster than 6 pixels per frame.
-- This prevents the player from accelerating infinitely. It acts as a speed limit.

set the friction to 1.
-- EXPLANATION: Friction is 0.85. Each frame, horizontal speed is multiplied by this number.
-- Since 0.85 is less than 1, the player gradually slows down when you stop pressing movement keys.
-- A value of 1 would mean no friction (sliding forever). A value of 0 would mean instant stop.

set the coyote_time to 100.
-- EXPLANATION: Coyote time is 100 milliseconds. This is a game-feel mechanic.
-- After the player walks off a ledge, they can still jump for 100ms.
-- Named after Wile E. Coyote who runs off cliffs and floats briefly before falling.
-- This makes the game feel more forgiving and responsive.

set the jump_buffer to 100.
-- EXPLANATION: Jump buffer is 100 milliseconds. Another game-feel mechanic.
-- If the player presses jump slightly BEFORE landing, the game remembers the press.
-- When they land within 100ms, the jump still happens. This prevents missed jumps.


-- SECTION: Air Control
-- These variables control how different movement feels in the air vs on the ground.

set the ground_acceleration to 1.
-- EXPLANATION: Ground acceleration is 0.5. When on the ground, pressing a direction
-- adds 0.5 to the player's horizontal speed each frame. This gives snappy ground control.

set the air_acceleration to 0.
-- EXPLANATION: Air acceleration is 0.25. When airborne, pressing a direction
-- only adds 0.25 per frame. This is HALF the ground value.
-- This means the player has less control in the air, which feels more realistic.

set the air_friction to 1.
-- EXPLANATION: Air friction is 0.95. In the air, the player retains more momentum.
-- Compare to ground friction of 0.85. The player slides more in the air.
-- This creates a difference between tight ground movement and floaty air movement.


-- SECTION: Dash Ability
-- The dash is a burst of speed that makes the player temporarily invincible.

set the dash_speed to 15.
-- EXPLANATION: During a dash, the player moves at 15 pixels per frame.
-- Compare to max_speed of 6. The dash is 2.5 times faster than normal movement.
-- This burst of speed lets players dodge through fire hazards.

set the dash_duration to 150.
-- EXPLANATION: The dash lasts 150 milliseconds (about 1/7 of a second).
-- During this time, the player moves at dash_speed and cannot be hurt by fire.
-- Short enough to require skill, long enough to be useful.

set the dash_cooldown to 500.
-- EXPLANATION: After dashing, the player must wait 500 milliseconds (half a second)
-- before dashing again. This prevents spam-dashing through every obstacle.
-- The cooldown creates a risk/reward decision: when should I use my dash?


-- SECTION: Wall Jump and Wall Slide
-- These variables control interactions with vertical surfaces (walls).

set the wall_slide_speed to 2.
-- EXPLANATION: When sliding down a wall, the player falls at only 2 pixels per frame.
-- Compare to normal falling with gravity (which accelerates). Wall sliding is much slower.
-- This gives the player time to wall-jump before falling too far.

set the wall_jump_force_x to 8.
-- EXPLANATION: When wall-jumping, the player is pushed horizontally at 8 pixels per frame.
-- This pushes them AWAY from the wall they jumped off of.
-- Without this force, the player would jump straight up along the wall.

set the wall_jump_force_y to 0.
subtract 12 from the wall_jump_force_y.
-- EXPLANATION: Wall jump vertical force is negative 12.
-- Slightly weaker than a normal jump (negative 13).
-- Combined with the horizontal push, this creates a diagonal leap off the wall.

set the wall_cling_time to 150.
-- EXPLANATION: When first touching a wall, the player "sticks" for 150 milliseconds.
-- This brief pause gives the player time to react and press jump.
-- Without it, the player would immediately start sliding and might miss the jump window.


-- SECTION: Gamepad Support
-- These variables handle game controller input.

set the gamepad_deadzone to 0.
-- EXPLANATION: The deadzone is 0.15. Analog sticks on controllers are rarely perfectly centered.
-- They might report tiny values like 0.02 even when untouched. The deadzone ignores
-- any stick input below 0.15 (15 percent), preventing unwanted drift.

set the gamepad_move_threshold to 1.
-- EXPLANATION: The move threshold is 0.5. The player only starts moving when the stick
-- is pushed past 50 percent of its range. This prevents accidental movement.


-- SECTION: Input Smoothing
-- This variable prevents jittery movement from rapid key presses.

set the input_smoothing to 0.
-- EXPLANATION: Input smoothing is 0.3. Instead of instantly changing direction,
-- the input gradually transitions from one value to another.
-- This is done using "linear interpolation" (lerp). Each frame, the input moves
-- 30 percent closer to the target value. This creates smooth, natural-feeling movement.


-- SECTION: Canvas Dimensions
-- These define the game window size in pixels.

set the canvas_width to 800.
-- EXPLANATION: The game screen is 800 pixels wide.

set the canvas_height to 600.
-- EXPLANATION: The game screen is 600 pixels tall.
-- Together, 800 by 600 defines the playing field where all gameplay happens.
-- All platform positions, fire positions, and goal positions are relative to this space.
